<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maghreb</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maghrebi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maghreb is the region of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> comprising present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, which unified it under one rule in the twelfth century. The Maghreb was linked by trans-Saharan trade to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> empires of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maghreb.jpg" alt="Maghreb" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Connormah · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maghreb_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The word maghrib is an Arabic term that the first Muslim Arab settlers gave in the 7th century to the recently conquered area west of the Umayyad capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. It means both the western land and the place where the sun sets, in contrast to the Mashriq in the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> isolate its northern parts from the rest of the continent, and these have long had commercial and cultural ties across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to Southern Europe and Western Asia.</p><p>The extent of the term has changed. Before the 20th century it most commonly meant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a>, often with eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and never with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. It has since widened to include Mauritania and the disputed territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>. During the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> its Muslim and Jewish inhabitants were known to Europeans as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> people have inhabited western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> since at least 10,000 BCE. Trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> dates back to the 1st millennium BCE, and tradition holds that they founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> around 800 BCE. The region was somewhat unified under the kingdom of Numidia, then briefly under the Kingdom of Mauretania, and then under Roman rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> invaded afterwards, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> briefly re-established a weak Roman rule.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> reached the Maghreb in early Umayyad times. The founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in 667 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> its first permanent foothold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> fell in 698 and the rest of the region by 709. The Umayyad, Abbasid and Fatimid caliphates held power, but the longest-lasting rule was that of local dynasties such as the Idrisids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, Zirids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a>. Trans-Saharan trade in salt, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves was costly and dangerous but highly profitable. In the 19th and early 20th centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> administered the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> followed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Jewish communities have a long presence there. The earliest documented one dates to the third century BCE, when Ptolemaic rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, and after the expulsion of Jews from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1492 many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The region is primarily inhabited by peoples of Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> ancestry, and couscous was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Intangible Cultural Heritage list in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maghreb” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibrahim's charge covered what is now eastern Algeria, Tunisia and Tripolitania, and in theory the whole <b>Maghreb</b> west of Cyrenaica, since the territory granted him was never demarcated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He returned to the <b>Maghreb</b> preaching strict tawhid, the absolute unity of God, and denouncing the ruling Almoravid dynasty as anthropomorphists who ascribed physical attributes to God and tolerated moral laxity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A small Saharan Berber military elite was trying to govern a large and highly cultured Andalusian and <b>Maghrebi</b> society.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The caliphate minted gold dinars from its first years, and its trade linked the Mediterranean, the <b>Maghreb</b> and, through Saharan routes, the gold of West Africa, while slaves from Europe were imported in large numbers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Málaga, Almería and Granada exported silk, dried figs and raisins, almonds and sugar and imported grain, often through Genoese merchants who kept a powerful presence in Málaga, and trade also ran across the Strait to the <b>Maghreb</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1207, after the Almohad caliph Muhammad al-Nasir had driven the Banu Ghaniya, Almoravid holdouts who had overrun much of the eastern <b>Maghreb</b>, out of Tunis, he appointed Abu Hafs’s son Abu Muhammad Abd al-Wahid governor of Ifriqiya, roughly modern Tunisia and eastern Algeria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
